-# Design analysis for the D1 variant
-# ----------------------------------
-# a) Concrete behavior and trace:
-#    The original task uses an edge palette with an outer border-color line and an inner fill-color
-#    line; the solver zips the two lines cell-by-cell. This variant keeps the same palette footprint,
-#    but the inner line is displayed in reverse order. The semantic fill sequence is therefore not the
-#    literal adjacent cells under/next to each border key. Example on the top edge:
-#      outer line  = [2, 4, 6]
-#      inner line  = [8, 1, 3]   (displayed on-grid)
-#      logical fill sequence = reverse(inner) = [3, 1, 8]
-#      mapping = 2->3, 4->1, 6->8
-#    Under the original solver, the same palette would mean 2->8, 4->1, 6->3. The change only affects
-#    palette decoding; hole detection and filling remain the same.
-# b) Complexity calibration:
-#    The palette still has 2-4 entries and uses two full lines, so the task stays compact and repeated
-#    evidence remains available across 2-4 sprites. Using reversed order is visually observable because
-#    at least one filled sprite is forced to use a non-middle palette position whose new fill differs
-#    from the direct cell-by-cell pairing.
-# c) Assumption audit against the original main():
-#    - _best_palette originally relied on the outer line having unique non-bg colors; that still holds.
-#    - The old _palette_mapping assumed "same column/row => same pair"; that assumption is false here
-#      and must be replaced by "same index in the outer sequence and reversed inner sequence".
-#    - _fill_enclosed_bg_regions only needs a valid border->fill dict and does not care how it was built,
-#      so its logic remains correct.
-# d) Placement legality:
-#    The generator still places one isolated 2xN/Nx2 palette on an edge and 2-4 separated hollow
-#    sprites with a 1-cell moat from each other and from the palette. Output-first generation is kept:
-#    render filled holes first, clear them back to bg for the input, then verify with main().
-# e) Visual observability:
-#    If the inner line were read in the same direction as the outer line, the task would visually collapse
-#    back to the original direct pairing. Reversing the displayed fill line makes the vertical/horizontal
-#    neighbor pair wrong for most entries, so the rule change is visible in a few examples.
-# f) Visual cleanliness:
-#    No extra markers, extra objects, or overlapping legends are introduced. The only visual novelty is
-#    the reversed order of the inner palette line, so the grids remain easy to parse.
-# g) Color inference:
-#    Border keys, fill values, and the distractor sprite color are all distinct. This avoids accidental
-#    same-color hints and keeps the new order-based mapping legible.
-# h) New main() assumptions and generator guarantees:
-#    - Palette outer and inner lines contain the same number of unique non-bg entries.
-#    - The solver reads the outer line in edge order (left->right or top->bottom) and the inner line in
-#      the opposite order.
-#    - At least one filled sprite uses a palette position changed by the reversal, so the generated sample
-#      does not degenerate to the original direct-mapping behavior.
 
 
